TODO (legal) — questions to settle before deciding whether these texts stay
public, and before any Zenodo deposit that includes them:

  a) Protection and owners. Which texts are protected works at all (a short
     headline may not be), and who holds the rights? Some publishers offer
     republication or Creative Commons terms (NOTICE.md section 1). Do those
     terms already cover this use?

  b) Basis for collecting and keeping them. Italian law has two
     text-and-data-mining exceptions, introduced by D.Lgs. 177/2021 to
     implement Directive (EU) 2019/790 arts. 3-4:
       - L. 633/1941 art. 70-ter: research organisations and
         cultural-heritage institutions, for the purposes of scientific
         research.
       - Art. 70-quater: anyone with lawful access, unless the rights holder
         has expressly reserved TDM. Copies may be kept only as long as the
         extraction needs them.
     Does either apply to this project? Have any of the publishers reserved
     TDM (opt-out)? See also art. 70-septies (added by L. 132/2025), on AI.

  c) Publishing. The exceptions in (b) concern reproduction and extraction.
     Does any of them, or quotation under art. 70, cover making the texts
     PUBLICLY available in a git repository or a Zenodo deposit, especially
     the full article bodies (texts over 5,000 characters)? Does the bulk
     collection engage the sui generis database right of a publisher's site
     or feed (Directive 96/9/EC art. 7; L. 633/1941 art. 102-bis)?

  d) Other jurisdictions. Several publishers are outside the EU (for
     example dawn.com, mb.com.ph, thehill.com).

  e) Personal data in the texts. What is the GDPR Art. 6 lawful basis? Do
     the national exemptions adopted under GDPR Art. 85 apply (in Italy,
     D.Lgs. 196/2003 artt. 136 ff.)?

  f) What to do now. The options include:
       - keep the texts as they are;
       - keep only hashes, lengths, timestamps, links and the derived signal
         values;
       - truncate the texts to headline + short summary;
       - make the texts private and purge them from git history.
     The purge rewrites history with a force-push. It needs the maintainer's
     explicit approval, and it must happen before any Zenodo deposit of the
     repository.

Takedown (PROPOSED, not yet in force): while this is unsettled, a publisher
who wants its texts removed could write to technical@cats-system.org, and
the texts would be removed from the current tree. Removing them from git
history needs the purge in (f).
